1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:10,240
Hey, you're listening to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing subscription app businesses.

2
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:14,560
We'll share insider secrets from the top subscription apps on the app stores.

3
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:15,840
Let's get into the show.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:23,120
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Biding.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,560
Our guest today is Cliff Weitzman, the founder and CEO of Speechify.

6
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:35,040
As someone with dyslexia, Cliff built Speechify to help himself learn by having text read out loud.

7
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:42,320
Cliff went on to blitz scale Speechifyy with a reverent approach to SaaS norms and a willingness to experiment.

8
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:54,240
On the podcast, we talk with Cliff about the benefits of building for a niche, the one ad that changed things for Speechify, and why Cliff is now hiring comedians.

9
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:56,960
Hey Cliff, thanks for being on the podcast.

10
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:00,480
I wanted to kick it off with the story of Speechify.

11
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:04,800
I know the story, but I don't know that all of our listeners do, and it's a good one.

12
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,840
So tell us why you built Speechify.

13
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:10,800
For sure, David, great to hop on with you.

14
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,000
Jacob, great to see you again.

15
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:13,440
Happy to share.

16
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:14,640
So I'm originally from Israel.

17
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:15,680
I grew up there.

18
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:22,560
I got to the US when I was thirteen and I'm severely dyslexic, which means that first, second, third, fourth grade, I kind of fake my way through school.

19
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,080
I was very bad at reading.

20
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:26,240
I finished reading my first book, I think, in eighth grade.

21
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:27,680
I couldn't spell my last name.

22
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:31,840
And my dad essentially eventually was like, Okay, I got five kids.

23
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:41,680
Tyler, who is Cliff's younger brother, started coding when he was nine, learned himself assembly in fifth grade, skipped four and a half years of math at Exeter and eventually did a master in AI at Stanford.

24
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:43,840
Alex, my sister, speaks seven languages.

25
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:44,960
And Cliff can't read.

26
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:46,960
So something has to give here.

27
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:48,880
So eventually my dad ended up reading books to me.

28
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,800
And the book I wanted to read the most was Harry Potter.

29
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:54,320
And so he would record himself on a cassette tape and I would just listen to that thing over and over.

30
00:01:54,480 --> 00:02:02,000
And when we moved to the US, US, I learned English by listening to Harry Potter audiobooks 22 times in a row and became obsessed with audiobooks.

31
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,840
That means I've been listening to about two audiobooks a week, every week, for the last sixteen years, about 100 books per year.

32
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,160
And I listen fast, as you could see by my speaking speed.

33
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,000
So I'm trying to modulate myself.

34
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:16,400
When I got to college, I found that most of my textbooks at Brown didn't have an audiobook.

35
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:20,160
And so I built a text to speech tool that would read out everything to me.

36
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:23,760
It could scan physical books with my phone and OCR them and read them.

37
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:28,720
I could with one tap import a 100 page PDF and turn it into a 2 hour audiobook.

38
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:28,880
We built a two hour audiobook.

39
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,680
Later, we built a Chrome extension that allows you to read your emails and Medium, and we created an API.

40
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:41,360
And I built this big followership of moms of other kids like me who have dyslexia, who got the book originally for their kids.

41
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:53,280
And I'd fly around the country to different schools that were for people who are neuro, you know, atypical, so people with dyslexia, ADHD, low vision, autism, commotion, anxiety, people who are second language speakers of English.

42
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,400
Today, that's about twenty percent, five percent of our user base, seventy five percent are neurotypical people.

43
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,000
So doctors, lawyers, accountants, people in the military, executives, people in finance.

44
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:04,640
And it's been a wild ride.

45
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:05,200
What?

46
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:12,000
Frame it like how long ago was that when you were flying around doing these like traveling salesman stuff?

47
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,120
Flying around was like three and a half, four years ago.

48
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:24,240
And really the way it started was I was so I had the best time as a student in college, more than anyone I've ever met.

49
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,600
I don't smoke, but I dance harder than anyone else.

50
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:33,440
And I got to do an independent concentration in renewable energy engineering, which is a mix of physics engineering and computer science.

51
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,200
I did a lot of design classes at RISD.

52
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:40,960
And so I convinced two of my professors to let me stay in college as a visiting scholar, which meant I didn't pay tuition.

53
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:46,560
I didn't do homework, but I got to live on the campus, be on meal plan and just like with classes when I felt like it.

54
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,360
And I was working on side projects.

55
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:54,800
I built about thirty six products when I was a student, everything from 3D printed skateboard breaks to iPhone apps and websites.

56
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:55,920
And that's how I paid for school.

57
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:00,880
And when I graduated, above all, my thesis was I wanted to be the person that I needed most when I was young.

58
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:03,840
And the thing I really needed when I was little was someone to read my books to me.

59
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:14,800
At the same time, I noticed that there was a huge shift in the application of narrow deep learning algorithms to speech synthesis, optical character recognition, transcription, translation, natural language processing and recommendation engines.

60
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:16,720
So I pulled that all together into one thing.

61
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:20,320
And I started messaging principles of schools for children with disabilities.

62
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:23,840
And I went to this one interview and the guy was like, cool app kid.

63
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:25,120
And he was really useful.

64
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,400
And he was like, I gotta go.

65
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:27,520
I I'm going to this conference.

66
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:28,800
And I was like, what's the conference?

67
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:31,840
He's like, well, it's the International Dyslexia Association conference in Florida.

68
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:33,280
And I was like, sounds like I should be there.

69
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:33,680
When is it?

70
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:35,520
And he's like, well, it's tomorrow at 8 a.m.

71
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:36,240
And I was like, cool.

72
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:36,960
So at 3 a.m.

73
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:37,840
I booked a ticket.

74
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:39,280
I had no money at the time.

75
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:42,640
So I convinced United to give me a discount of 40% on the phone.

76
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:44,800
Booked an Airbnb near the hotel.

77
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:51,120
And then when the keynote speaker finished speaking, I jumped on the stage, grabbed the microphone, and demoed Speechify.

78
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,760
And ACE offered to fly me out to teach their kids how to use Speechify.

79
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:54,720
And that's how everything started.

80
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:57,120
Wait, wait, did you get thrown out of of the conference?

81
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,080
What happened after that?

82
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,880
I didn't get thrown out of the conference.

83
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:02,560
They adopted me as one of their own.

84
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:07,040
They were like, this is such a cute 21 year old who's like, thinks he's a traveling salesman.

85
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:10,400
And is so open about the fact that he has dyslexia.

86
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:17,520
I actually just got a message two days ago from Ben Powers, who is the principal of one of the biggest, most successful schools for kids with learning differences.

87
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:25,200
And I'm now building an online assessment that will automatically tell you, heuristically, the probability of you having dyslexia or ADHD.

88
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,160
And he's been helping me actually figure out some of the components of that.

89
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:29,040
That's fascinating.

90
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,640
That's such an amazing story.

91
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,280
You know, I think too many app developers don't.

92
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:37,280
just think about the basic marketing principles.

93
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:43,040
Like you went to a conference where your target market was and you spoke to them.

94
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:51,600
And that was, and we were talking earlier, like you saying, you know, staying in the niche early on, you feel like was a really strong point.

95
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:57,120
Tell me more about that and how marketing in that niche really helped you grow in those early days.

96
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:57,840
Yeah.

97
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,880
So number one, I'm obsessed with text of speech.

98
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,960
I think it's the coolest technology that's ever been built.

99
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:05,360
I wouldn't be the person that I am today without text of speech.

100
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:09,360
There's a book called Shantaram about a guy who is addicted to drugs in Australia.

101
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:13,680
He runs away from prison, like breaks out, goes, becomes a slum doctor in India.

102
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:17,040
He meets all these, you know, drug lords and he talks, he gets to know them all.

103
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:19,040
He's like, man, I'm really like a drug lord.

104
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:22,720
Like my core skill set is I'm a really good leader and I don't give up.

105
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:26,400
I just happened to be lucky that audio books were a thing when I was a kid.

106
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:32,000
So I became really erudite and I used text of speech to like read all of Aristotle and Wikipedia and all that stuff.

107
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,400
Um, and that's why I did well in university.

108
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:39,120
Um, and, you know, that kicked me into, into working on Speechify.

109
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:41,840
Um, and, and then from there, you know, I just kept building it.

110
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:42,560
I forgot the question.

111
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:43,280
Say it again.

112
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:45,280
What's it?

113
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,800
Um, staying really niche earlier early, focused more on dyslexia, really helped you kind of get track of it.

114
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:52,480
Don't worry, don't, don't sweat it.

115
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:53,600
I do that like three times a day.

116
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:56,160
So, that was exactly right.

117
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:01,440
So, what was really motivating about staying niche in this regard is in the beginning, I was the core developer.

118
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:04,080
So, you upload a PDF, it wouldn't work.

119
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:09,520
You tried it again, the app would crash, like it takes like four or five attempts before like a good outcome came out.

120
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,800
So, if you didn't have dyslexia, you're like, see a guy, see a buddy.

121
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:17,360
But if you had dyslexia, you'd try five times because you're not going to read the book yourself.

122
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:26,320
And after the fifth time it works, to this day, fifteen percent of the reviews on the App Store are people who say they cried when they downloadaded the app because it was so transformative for them.

123
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:34,720
And so I was allowed to produce a more shoddy product because the need that I was serving for that particular cohort was so acute.

124
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:42,160
And the next centric circle outside of dyslexia was ADHD, and then low vision and autism and commotion and anxiety and second language learners.

125
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:43,680
And so I realized this.

126
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:51,840
And so when I started marketing Speechify, what I did is I identified five Facebook groups for moms of kids with learning differences, specifically dyslexia.

127
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,520
And then I did another five for ADHD.

128
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:58,560
I found the Reddit groups and I set a goal that I would post on all of them.

129
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:05,680
And I messaged, you know, six of my best friends and I was like, if I don't post in every single one of these groups by, you know, 10 a.m.

130
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:08,160
on Monday, I have to run 10 miles this weekend.

131
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:09,840
So I like high accountability.

132
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:14,880
So I posted and actually it became a problem because Reddit only lets you post up to a certain amount that it takes your bot.

133
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:16,800
So I had to open multiple Reddit accounts to do it.

134
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:22,640
And don't worry, none of the, none of the Reddit Terms of Service police are here today.

135
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:23,120
So, okay.

136
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:24,400
And I was like, cool.

137
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:25,680
Well, how do I keep engaging them?

138
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:30,560
So I ended up starting publicly on the internet a book about my experience being dyslexic.

139
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:32,000
I'd write 500 words a day.

140
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:35,840
And it made me, you know, really good at communicating with this cohort.

141
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:40,240
And it's really life transformationative for all these people.

142
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:45,920
Now the phone was recorded sound was invented by Edison.

143
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:49,040
People don't know Edison was like 90% deaf.

144
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:53,920
The reason why he was the inventor of recorded sound is because he could not perceive sound well.

145
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:58,160
So he would have to put his hand on the wood to feel the vibrations, and then he goes, you know what?

146
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:01,280
What if we put a pin on this drum and we see how it oscillates up and down?

147
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:06,240
So the critical invention behind the phone was invented by a guy who had a disability.

148
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:08,160
And this is actually true for a lot of inventions.

149
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:13,440
They start with someone who is optimizing for a small marginalized group, and at the end of the day, it actually helps everyone.

150
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:18,960
So, you know, over the last five years, AirPods heralded a shift in how people consume information.

151
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:23,600
The same thing for double speed Netflix and YouTube and, you know, lecture capture over the pandemic.

152
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:28,160
And so people just learned how to listen fast on podcasts, on audiobooks.

153
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:29,840
And so I just focused on that group.

154
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:35,200
And I was like, my number one mission above everything else in life is to be the person that I needed most when I was young.

155
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:39,680
I want to make sure that reading is never a barrier for learning for anyone, no matter if you have dyslexia or ADD.

156
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:46,000
If you grew up in Ghana and there were fifty kids in your third grade class and you didn't get individualized attention, like that shouldn't matter if you want to learn.

157
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:48,320
I'm going to personally go and remove that challenge for you.

158
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:51,360
And then if we can help everyone else, sweet, we'll do that too.

159
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:56,160
How did you, how did so at some point, yeah, you talk about moving out in these concentric circles.

160
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:12,000
At some point you were like, okay, I'm done posting on, you know, folks with special needs, like forums, and you were like, okay, like, I'm ready to go to, like, college students or whatever, whatever you next like, you said it was ADHD or what, but even like beyond that, I mean, Specify's a product, everyone can get value out of it, right, if you understand how to use it.

161
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:14,880
Like, when did you decide, like, was it like growth driven?

162
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:16,800
Was you just like, okay, it's time to go to the next level?

163
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:24,080
Like, there must have been something driving you beyond, because if it was just to help folks, you know, in the same predicament, you would have just stopped there, but you kept going, right?

164
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:28,240
So, like, how did you, you know, decide to, like, push out and like, what was your motivation?

165
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:37,440
So when I was a student, I got provisioned a bunch of different pieces of software to help me with dyslexia, and they all were horrible, like so bad.

166
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:43,200
So I was cobbling together my own tools that worked fast, that had better voices, that had all the stuff that I wanted.

167
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:49,760
And I realized pretty quickly that if you want to build something that actually makes an impact, selling to educational institutions is not the way to do it.

168
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:58,880
Now, I feel that we have been blessed to be alive in 2020 where consumer subscription is an acceptable business model, right?

169
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:06,000
And that was allowed for us as Spotify and Netflix educated the consumer that it's okay to pay a subscription for software.

170
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:08,480
Now, actually, they pay for media.

171
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:12,160
In my case, they pay for software, which is amazing because I don't have any cogs for the most part.

172
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:13,920
I think it's just server.

173
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:15,440
So that's great.

174
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:17,040
And I was like, cool.

175
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,400
I'm not going to have fun selling to schools.

176
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:21,600
I would lose all my hair.

177
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:29,760
So instead, what I'm going to do is I'm going to build a powerful megaphone so everyone will learn about speechifying.

178
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:33,280
And if you happen to have a friend with dyslexia, you will tell them about it.

179
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:34,480
So I'll give you an example.

180
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:39,840
On the car ride on the way here, I got a message from a friend who's like, hey, I just talked to Sarah Blakely, who is the founder of Spanx.

181
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:43,120
She has a she'd love to meet you because she has some connection to dyslexia.

182
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:44,800
This happens all the time.

183
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,560
People could I had our call earlier today, you know, hey Cliff, great to meet you.

184
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:50,000
By the way, I have to ask a favor.

185
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:52,880
My best friend, her daughter was recently diagnosed with dyslexia.

186
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:54,000
Can you hopp on a call with her?

187
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:54,560
Absolutely.

188
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:55,600
This is what I'm here for.

189
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:57,200
And so I was like, cool.

190
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:03,440
If I educate everyone that this exists and I also tell my story, they will introduce it to other friends that they have with dyslexia.

191
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:04,480
And that's exactly what happened.

192
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:05,840
Now, how did I do this?

193
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,080
I ended up making an email list in Excel in Google Sheets.

194
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:14,720
I wrote a Google Sheets list of the top 100 best performing consumer subscription companies in the world.

195
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:17,440
And I emailed every CEO, every head of growth.

196
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,440
And then I hopped on Zoom calls with them.

197
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:22,400
And I'd fly to wherever they were in the world to work with them.

198
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:33,840
So I got to know the founders of Reflectly and Blinkist and Dollar Shave Club and Honey and Audible and Grammarly and Whoop and Neil from Lightrix.

199
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:40,240
And then I literally sat behind them in their office and looked at how they bought Instagram ads.

200
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:44,160
And then I moved to LA where there were a lot of really good e commerce companies.

201
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:46,080
And then I got really good at buying.

202
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:47,520
People were okay with this.

203
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:49,520
You're just like, hey, I'm just a random internet guy.

204
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:52,560
I want to come sit in your office and watch you, watch you do Instagram ads.

205
00:12:53,040 --> 00:13:00,720
You know, it starts with a cold Instagram or Facebook or email saying, hey, okay, I'm flying to Vancouver soon to meet with the founder of Lululemon.

206
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:02,720
I've been messaging this guy for three and a half years.

207
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:04,560
Finally, this meeting is happening.

